# Footprint

What tig records about how a project was built, and how it keeps it safe.

## Objects and trees

Every file is stored once, as an object named by the SHA-256 of its content. Objects are stored plain, so Git delta-compresses them along with the project, and every read checks the content against the name: a tampered object is refused. A tree is one folder of the project, one line per entry; a folder that did not change is the same tree, so comparing two trees skips everything that did not change. Code: [objects.go](../tig/objects.go), [tree.go](../tig/tree.go), [record.go](../tig/record.go).

Which files: in a Git repository, Git's own list (tracked, and untracked but not ignored); otherwise every file, without `.git`, `.tig` and the folders the caller skips. A local cache of file sizes and times keeps unchanged files from being hashed again. Code: [files.go](../tig/files.go), [statcache.go](../tig/statcache.go).

## Snapshots

A snapshot records the project's tree, the snapshot before it, when, why and by whom, and how the documents' text blocks changed since (see [the chain](chain.md)). A snapshot is taken only when something changed. Code: [snapshot.go](../tig/snapshot.go), [repo.go](../tig/repo.go).

Putting files back from a snapshot refuses any path that could land outside the project or inside `.git` or `.tig`, Windows names such as `.. ` or `a:b` included, and never writes through a symbolic link, because trees can come from someone else's repository.

## Works and steps

A work is one construction: the text blocks it is for, what it was meant to do, the snapshots before and after it, its steps in order, and its result. A step is one thing done in it:

| Kind | What tig records |
|---|---|
| `edit` | the trees before and after the step: exactly the files it changed |
| `command`, `install` | the command, and the trees around it when it changed files |
| `check` | a command run to check the design, its exit code and output, and who ran it (`tig` when tig ran it itself) |
| `decision` | a question and its answer |
| `note` | anything else |

The work in progress lives in `.tig/WORK`, which stays on the machine. Finishing it writes the steps, then the work, and moves the `WORKS` head. Without a result given, the result comes from the checks: `passed` when every command's last check passed, `failed` when one did not, `done` when there was no check. Code: [work.go](../tig/work.go).

Several processes can use one `.tig` at once: the heads move under a lock file, and so does the work in progress, so no record is lost. Code: [lock.go](../tig/lock.go).

